00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.890
Sergio and I both work in
an org at Google called

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.500
Cloud Engineering Productivity.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.660
This means essentially
our job at Google

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.530
is to build tools to help make
other Googlers more productive.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.100
Specifically, Sergio works
on a tool called Louhi,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.530
and I work on something
called GlassPane.

00:01:08.012 --> 00:01:09.970
SERGIO FELIX: To give
you a little more context

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.330
around this, Louhi is a
release orchestration tool.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.250
It basically allows
developers-- but mostly

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.680
release engineers--
a way to automate

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.210
the process of managing,
scheduling, planning,

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.900
controlling, software builds
through different stages

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.300
and deployments.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.880
Basically, you can think of
Louhi like most CI/CD tools

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.020
out there.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.840
There's a concept of a
flow that has triggers,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.370
and the triggers
kick off a pipeline

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:34.690
that has a bunch of steps.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.060
And then you get notifications
when those steps move along.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.880
And we use these
weird looking flows

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.630
to compile and build these
weird looking applications

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.630
that in our team, we
call hybrid applications.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.360
These are applications are
made up from both open source

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.600
software and internal software
that gets bundled together,

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.870
build tested, and so on.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.210
SOEREN WALLS: GlassPane,
on the other hand,

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.540
is an internal metrics
platform at Google

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.120
that's essentially designed
to help leaders understand

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.870
the health and relative
performance of their projects

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.809
and teams, through mainly
actionable insights.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.670
So today, we want to tell
you a story about our journey

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:23.180
and how we migrated
both Louhi and GlassPane

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.450
from monolithic applications
and split them up

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.921
into microservices.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.890
But first, why did we
decide to build though

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.570
Louhi and GlassPane
as monolithic apps

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.820
in the first place?

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.470
This allowed us to
experiment quickly.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.870
We needed to focus
on the business logic

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.940
and having a single
binary really

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:51.260
allowed us to do this while
preventing initial overdesign.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.240
We weren't as concerned
with everything

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:57.080
working as beautifully as
possible as long as it worked.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.480
Since we were a small
team, we could also easily

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.240
manage the changes
just kind of by saying,

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.340
you work on this thing.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.600
I'll work on this thing.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.350
It also made the maintenance
around our binary pretty easy

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.910
because we only
had to do it once.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:17.320
It was great until it wasn't.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.550
Our teams were growing.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.800
We started getting more
product requirements,

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.100
and we started onboarding
new team members.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.130
We started to have too
much cognitive load.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.110
When we brought a new
person onto the team,

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.810
instead of just needing to
understand the small piece they

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:34.182
were responsible for, they
had to spend a few weeks

00:03:34.182 --> 00:03:36.640
to figure out how the whole
architecture of our application

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.300
worked.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.920
We had to start
delaying deployments

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.330
to sync features across
the different team members,

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.420
and we had no way to
independently scale

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.880
certain small parts of
the app that might need

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.300
more resources than others.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.300
We had to scale the
whole app all at once,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.990
wasting tons of resources.

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.150
This made it really
messy to debug.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:01.790
When an error happened, it
was really hard to go and find

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.530
the root cause.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:07.700
We started to feel like we had
built a cage around ourselves

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.190
that we were
desperate to escape,

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:15.030
and this is where
microservices came in.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.160
SERGIO FELIX: But what is
a microservice and what

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.720
do they even come from?

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.880
Before we go deep into
patterns and all that,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.620
we want to take a
step back and just

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.460
look at the history of
where microservices actually

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:27.990
come from.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.270
You will know about my
own monolithic apps.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.900
We have a single binary
that gets deployed.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.400
Changing one of its internal
services, as someone mentioned,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.070
requires the deployment
of the whole application.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.710
So that's a slow
velocity to production.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.840
And then a Soeren mentioned,
new engineers arrive,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.840
and they have to understand
these really complex

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:46.650
applications with
a large codebase.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.540
And they usually have
to submit PR requests.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.970
And there's a central
group of people, hopefully,

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.540
who have the authority
to approve those changes.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.790
Again, this is slow.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:01.330
They also have a single database
where all the modules inside

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:04.250
talk to the database.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.430
The industry then moved on to
service oriented architecture.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.115
The term was coined around 1998
by a group of people working

00:05:12.115 --> 00:05:13.490
on something called
[? coreby. ?]

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.100
Those old enough might
be able to remember.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.670
And the idea here was that
you would build services

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:22.760
a bit more decoupled then
[? in the ?] monolithic app.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.070
And you had four types
of business layer

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:27.830
at the top, a bunch of
enterprise services,

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.710
and at the bottom, application
infrastructure services-- all

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.168
tied together with this thing
called the enterprise service

00:05:33.168 --> 00:05:34.460
bus.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.280
Around that time, we
wanted to share everything,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.740
and so we had a single
database that was

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.780
shared across all the services.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:43.280
The problem with
this is that if you

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.250
made a change to your database,
that change could ripple

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.980
throughout all your services.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.487
That was a problem.

00:05:50.487 --> 00:05:52.070
Also the enterprise
service was needed

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.103
to scale depending on how
many services you had,

00:05:54.103 --> 00:05:55.770
and there was a single
point of failure.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.270
Then things evolve a little bit,
and we got to microservices.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.340
Here autonomous
teams are organized

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.440
over a single functionality.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.500
Microservices are
independently deployable,

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.850
and teams produce
and consume APIs.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:15.420
This makes it really
simple for other services

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.330
to scale really fast, and
it enables innovation.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:21.080
Also, nowadays, microservices
are deploying containers,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.800
so that it gets even
simpler to deploy.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:30.080
SOEREN WALLS: So what are the
benefits of microservices?

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.990
Well, they're pretty
simple to understand.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.320
These small services make it
so that onboarding new team

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.800
members is relatively easy when
compared to a monolithic app

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.750
because people are only
focused on the things

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:43.820
that they're responsible for.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:46.780
It also allows you to work
on potentially different tech

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.333
stacks within your team.

00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:50.000
You might have someone
working on Python

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.583
for this microservice,
someone working

00:06:51.583 --> 00:06:52.940
with Java for the other one--

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:54.960
pretty cool.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.480
They also have isolated
responsibility,

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.290
which means that all
the microservices are

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.060
responsible for a
single thing, and you

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:06.110
can deploy them independently.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:10.250
They're also scalable
and distributable,

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:13.280
and you can reuse business logic
throughout your application

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.670
simply by making a call
to a microservice that's

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:21.050
responsible for the certain
functionality that you need.

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.868
They're also really
fault tolerant.

00:07:22.868 --> 00:07:24.410
Later, we'll talk
about some patterns

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.320
you can use the circuit
breaker pattern to handle

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.250
when a service goes down.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.610
This is something that's
not quite as elegantly

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.870
possible with monolithic apps.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:37.070
OK, those are some
of the benefits

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:41.330
but how do I actually go about
migrating a monolithic app?

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.990
This is something that's really
daunting for a lot of teams.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.323
You might have a
bunch of legacy code.

00:07:47.323 --> 00:07:49.490
You might have all these
different dependencies that

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.550
are intermixed, and
you're wondering, OK, how

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.340
do I go about tackling this?

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:59.630
Well, with both
Louhi and GlassPane,

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.520
we had a monolithic
app that we wanted

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:06.890
to break into microservices,
but we weren't sure how.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.830
We initially asked ourselves
these questions, which you also

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:11.330
might be wondering.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.280
How big should these
services even be?

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.400
And how do I actually go about
splitting up the functionality

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.650
in my monolithic app?

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:25.580
Well, the micro part
of microservices--

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:27.890
it's kind of more of a
label than a description.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.440
The size of a microservice
can really vary.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.690
There's this concept called
service granularity that's

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.900
essentially how big
are your microservices,

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:40.250
and there's no one size
fits all approach for this.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:44.179
It's going to depend on your
infrastructure and your tech

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:46.230
stack.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.110
Ask yourself how much
overhead is associated

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:51.330
with building a new service.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.360
Consider things like source
control management, deployment,

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:55.733
testing, et cetera.

00:08:55.733 --> 00:08:57.150
Later, we'll talk
about some tools

00:08:57.150 --> 00:09:00.700
that you can use to
automate these things.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.940
Ask yourself, does
creating a service cost

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:06.370
more than implementing
its functionality?

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:08.920
If that's the case,
that's a good indication

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:12.630
your service might be too small.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.250
One approach that's
commonly used

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.710
is domain driven
design, which emphasizes

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:19.530
the idea of bounded
contexts where

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:21.810
each component of
your application--

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.120
or in this case,
each microservice--

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:28.260
is only responsible
for a single thing.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.750
If you're familiar with
object oriented design,

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:33.570
you can think of this as each
microservice handling one

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.880
strong entity and
its related business

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.360
logic in the data layer.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.478
Beware of an anti pattern
called nano services.

00:09:43.478 --> 00:09:46.020
This is essentially when your
services are so small that it's

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.888
not feasible to maintain them.

00:09:47.888 --> 00:09:49.680
To give you an example
of this, we actually

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.680
ran into this problem
with GlassPane.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:56.190
So it started as a monolithic
app running an App Engine.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:59.070
And after a design
process, we decided

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.520
that there were essentially
seven domains in GlassPane.

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.110
So we created seven
microservices.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:08.130
That was our magic number-ish.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:11.070
We moved from the
monolithic app first

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:13.440
to this core micro
service that talks

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.840
to six other
microservices that handle

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.800
their respective domains.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.120
And where we went
a little too small,

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.310
a little awry is when
we started making

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.970
an individual microservice
for every single metric

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:30.580
that we wanted to
show in GlassPane.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:34.590
Now we wanted to show hundreds
of metrics in GlassPane,

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:36.510
so with that
infrastructure, we would

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.660
have to build hundreds of
different microservices

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:42.660
and do all of their release
and testing and logging

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.460
and all of the things
associated with each of those,

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.990
along with the complicated
data logic and business logic

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:50.760
that we might have.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.580
Instead, we took a step back.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:55.770
We said, OK, this is
not feasible for a team

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.490
size and our tech stack.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:00.300
We decided we'll have a single
microservice responsible

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:01.620
for all the metrics.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:05.220
But how do we keep that isolated
responsibility principle?

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.490
Well, having a
single microservice

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:13.050
allowed us to only have
release and tests and logging

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:14.340
for a single one.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:15.720
So it solved that problem.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.100
But we were able to take
advantage of Google Cloud

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:22.080
Functions, which provided
that isolated responsibility

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.840
without all of the
maintenance that goes along

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.300
with those separate services.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:29.040
So this was our solution.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.510
And maybe something
similar could work for you.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.790
All right, I just
talked about deciding

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.250
on the size of
your microservices.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:41.250
But how do you actually go about
splitting up the functionality

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.650
in your monolithic app?

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:47.370
There's a pattern for this
called the Strangler pattern.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.310
It gets its name from the
Strangler vines of the fig

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:55.410
tree, which start at the top
of the tree in the leaves,

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:59.850
and they slowly work their way
down over years potentially

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:02.460
until they're
rooted in the soil.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:06.528
And they've entirely consumed
the tree, and it dies.

00:12:06.528 --> 00:12:08.570
You can think about
migrating your monolithic app

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.500
kind of like that--

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:16.330
slowly and creepily and
hopefully not in a forest.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.620
So to give you an
example, here's

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.460
a monolithic
application we've got.

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:21.840
It's a single binary.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.340
It's got some
modules in it-- maybe

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:25.890
the accounting module, the
shipping module, the inventory

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.170
module, and they're
all communicating

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:29.370
with the same database.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:33.060
And when a user makes a
request, the monolithic app

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:35.850
is the only service
that handles it.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.890
With the Strangler
pattern, the first step

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.920
to migrating this monolithic
app into microservices

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.095
is to add something
called a proxy.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.650
Initially, all it's going
to do is reroute requests

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:52.870
that are coming from your
user to the monolithic app.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:56.550
Nothing has changed about the
way your user is interacting

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:57.340
with the app.

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:58.892
This is just a
layer you've setup

00:12:58.892 --> 00:13:00.600
so that you can begin
redirecting traffic

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:04.250
to new services as
you implement them.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:07.100
Next let's say we want to
tackle the functionality that's

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:10.730
provided related to inventories
in our monolithic app.

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:12.890
We've decided that's a
domain that we want to put

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.810
into its own microservice.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.870
So we create a separate
microservice binary

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:19.970
that we call the
inventory microservice.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:23.810
And notice that it has its
own dedicated database.

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:25.370
This is crucial.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:28.130
Microservices are not
really microservices

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:32.610
unless the data
layers are separate.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:35.220
We haven't begun implementing
the functionality yet.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.290
We just created a binary.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.360
The next step is
to set up a link

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:44.730
between the proxy and that
new binary that's running.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.040
As we implement the
functionality related

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:50.310
to inventory, we can
begin using the proxy

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:54.000
to redirect some of the
traffic into that new inventory

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.920
microservice binary.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:58.210
Once we're done implementing
the functionality,

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:00.210
we can remove that
functionality from

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:02.490
the monolithic application.

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:04.950
Next, we tackle the next domain.

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:07.720
Let's say we want to
tackle the shipping logic.

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:09.150
So we create a new binary--

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:11.940
again, with its own database--

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:16.420
setup a link from the proxy to
that new microservice binary,

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:18.900
then as we implement the
functionality of the shipping

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:22.710
microservice, all the
traffic gets redirected.

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:24.360
And we can remove
that functionality

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:26.760
from our monolithic application.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.520
We do the same thing for
the remaining modules--

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.230
create a new binary, we get up
with the proxy, implement it.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:36.750
And now all of the
functionality is

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:38.310
gone from our monolithic app.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:43.030
And voila, you're in
microservices-- great.

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:44.910
SERGIO FELIX: For
Louhi, fortunately,

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:47.820
we started with something
a lot more simpler--

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:49.860
our initial monolithic
application that

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:53.850
was built with Python 2.5,
and it was running with Flask.

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:58.500
And because the way we
architected, sorry, our modules

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:02.760
to begin with, we could easily
translate basically each module

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:05.560
into a service in
our new architecture.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:08.920
So for the most part,
that was pretty easy.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:12.070
However, not all the
modules were simple.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:13.710
We had this pipeline
module, which

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:16.520
was kind of like a monster,
like a Frankenstein.

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:19.180
It did a bunch of things.

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:22.290
It stored the pipeline
configuration.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:25.470
It managed the states
of the pipelines.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.158
It managed a catalog
of stage types

00:15:28.158 --> 00:15:29.700
and then also
contained all the logic

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.987
to actually run the pipeline.

00:15:31.987 --> 00:15:33.570
So when we were
looking at this and we

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.300
wanted to transform this
module into microservices,

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:40.410
we decided to split this module
into three microservices.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:42.150
The pipeline
service just managed

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:46.073
the state and the configuration
of the pipeline, and that's it.

00:15:46.073 --> 00:15:47.490
And we had a
separate service that

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:49.830
just managed a catalog
of stage types,

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:53.120
and all the logic necessary
to run the actual stages

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:55.470
were in the GCB executor--

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.510
pretty simple.

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:03.400
SOEREN WALLS: All right,
so where to get started?

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:06.550
Well, source code and
documentation-- good

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:08.360
place to start.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:12.640
Design docs are going
to be your best friends.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:14.960
Create a strategy
for how you're going

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:19.660
to migrate from a monolithic
app into microservices.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:22.630
It can be a costly
refactoring process

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:25.330
that you might have to delay
feature development for a while

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:30.380
or take some resources from
other parts of your team to do.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:34.228
You want to create a design for
your final architecture, what

00:16:34.228 --> 00:16:35.270
you want it to look like.

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:39.230
Decide on those domains
that your application

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:41.030
needs to handle.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:42.530
This is actually
a cool opportunity

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:45.200
to maybe rethink some
of the things that

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:50.480
maybe weren't so pretty or
good about your initial app.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:52.850
In Louhi and GlassPane, we
also created design docs

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:55.940
for each individual
microservice.

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:59.720
This made it really clear
what the entities were

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:01.820
that were being handled
by each microservice,

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:03.380
the business logic
related to them,

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:07.339
the data layers, and more
importantly, the contracts that

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:09.740
were being established
between each microservice

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:11.795
and the other services
that it needed a consume

00:17:11.795 --> 00:17:14.910
or that needed to consume it.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:18.420
Next ask yourself, do you want
to split up your source code

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:20.609
repository?

00:17:20.609 --> 00:17:23.670
In GlassPane and Louhi,
we decided to do this.

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.520
By splitting our repository
into a single repository

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:28.710
per micro service,
we established

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:32.062
clear dependency relationships
between the services.

00:17:32.062 --> 00:17:34.020
This is a really cool
thing about microservices

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:36.620
to take advantage of.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:39.290
This also meant that,
essentially, there

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.570
is no other choice but to
deploy them separately,

00:17:41.570 --> 00:17:46.580
which is it's one of the
key benefits to get out.

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:50.155
Finally, set team
responsibilities.

00:17:50.155 --> 00:17:51.530
If you're a small
team, you might

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:53.270
have one member
who's responsible

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:54.710
for multiple microservices.

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:56.240
If you're a large
team, you might

00:17:56.240 --> 00:18:00.140
have whole sub teams dedicated
to a single microservice.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.000
Decide on this and
you'll be good to go.

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:07.500
You might be
wondering, OK, if I've

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:10.515
got a separate repository for
every microservice-- like here

00:18:10.515 --> 00:18:13.080
in GlassPane, you can
see we had a repository

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:15.390
for the core service, the
preference service, the team

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:16.700
service, and so on.

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:18.900
How do I share
code between them?

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:22.530
Our solution was to add
a common repository.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:23.850
It's not a microservice.

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:25.770
It's just a separate
repository where

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:29.100
we can put shared code that all
the individual microservices

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:30.180
can depend on.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:32.970
This means we can still deploy
them separately and test them

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:38.952
separately, and they're not
depending on each other.

00:18:38.952 --> 00:18:40.910
SERGIO FELIX: OK, so we
thought about the sizes

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:42.110
of the services.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:44.480
We have our documents
set up in place.

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:48.630
And then for us in Louhi and
in GlassPane, we were thinking,

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.090
we want to move
to microservices.

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:54.030
What technology
stack do we pick?

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:56.630
So you can run microservices
in multiple ways.

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:58.790
You can run them as App
Engine applications,

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:02.240
as a customized Compute
Engine, in Kubernetes,

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:04.460
in Cloud Functions, in K native.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:05.930
It depends.

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:09.920
For us, specifically,
we picked GKE mainly

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:13.695
because we could deploy a wide
variety of applications in GKE.

00:19:13.695 --> 00:19:15.770
If you can put it
in a container,

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:18.530
you can probably run it.

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:21.590
We were also super interested
in self-healing capabilities

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:24.410
and the auto scaling
capabilities in GKE.

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:29.090
This meant our application would
be up and running all the time.

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:33.090
Also we really liked the
declared configuration in GKE.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:35.090
We could just basically
tell GKE what we wanted,

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:38.090
and GKE will get it done.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:41.930
Also we really like that in
GKE, we had easy network setup,

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:45.350
and GKE basically handled
the updates of our Kubernetes

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:46.367
cluster for us.

00:19:46.367 --> 00:19:48.200
So we didn't have to
manually scheduled this

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:50.180
and run it for ourselves.

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:53.540
Plus since our old application
already leveraged most

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:56.000
of the GCP services,
we were also interested

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:01.820
because GKE can leverage things
like Stackdriver, Pub/Sub,

00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:04.210
Datastore, and so on.

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:06.620
For the back end, though,
as our team changed,

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:10.480
we were more experts
on Java than in Python,

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:13.190
and so we picked Spring.

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:14.705
Mainly because we knew Java.

00:20:14.705 --> 00:20:16.730
Our developers knew Spring.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:18.320
It's a perfect fit.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.020
Spring is a very
mature framework

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:21.470
used throughout the industry.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:24.080
It's open source, cloud native.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:26.090
It's as flexible as
you want it to be.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:27.680
If you want to, you
can specifically

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:32.090
configure something or let
Spring auto configure itself.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:33.690
Also there's tons
of documentation.

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:37.350
It was really easy
to get started.

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:39.160
On top of that, we
found that there

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:43.350
are these two projects, Spring
Cloud GCP and Spring Cloud

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:44.715
Kubernetes.

00:20:44.715 --> 00:20:46.840
The first project basically
allows your Springboard

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:50.170
application to leverage
things like Pub/Sub, DataStore

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:52.990
authentication, and
so on really easily.

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.910
And Spring Cloud
Kubernetes allowed

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:57.970
the Springboard binaries
running on a GKE cluster

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:00.760
to leverage the Kubernetes API.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:02.860
We'll talk about how we
use both of these things

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:03.940
in other slides.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:09.060
SOEREN WALLS: Both
GlassPane pane and Louhi

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.190
use this front end
framework called

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:13.560
Polymer for our front end UI.

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.080
You might be wondering, why
am I talking about a front end

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.260
framework in a presentation
about microservices?

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:21.120
Well, Polymer takes
advantage of something

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:24.930
called Web Components
where each piece of the UI

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:29.250
is a separate component with
isolated responsibility.

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:31.290
It allows us to think
about our front end

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:33.850
in much the same way we think
about our microservices back

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:36.887
end architecture.

00:21:36.887 --> 00:21:38.220
SERGIO FELIX: Here's an example.

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:41.750
This is the navigation bar
or the side bar for Louhi,

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:44.690
and you can clearly
see how it's composed

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:47.300
of tiny pieces of components
that interact together

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:50.755
to create a single-page
application.

00:21:50.755 --> 00:21:52.130
We encourage
everyone to go check

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:54.830
out the Polymer Project
and the [? lead ?] project.

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:55.980
They're pretty interesting.

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:57.740
We found it useful.

00:21:57.740 --> 00:22:02.660
So anyway, now we have an idea
of how to speed our services.

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:04.670
We've picked that technology.

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:06.500
And now we want to
be able to expose

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:08.030
these services to our clients--

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:11.620
our front ends or
their applications.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:14.600
I'm going to talk about two
patterns you could use--

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:18.060
gateways and aggregator
or composite services.

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:22.130
Now gateways, as the name
implies, they act as a door.

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:24.620
They basically
decouple your clients

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:27.320
from your downstream services.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:30.530
Gateways that basically allow
you to configure an optimal

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:33.840
API for each of your clients.

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:37.110
It also allows you to aggregate
calls to the services.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:38.930
So your front end
doesn't need to know

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:43.010
that in order to get a flow, it
needs to go to three services.

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:47.270
The gateway can handle
that for the client.

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:50.030
Also, the gateway allows you
to set up throttling rules

00:22:50.030 --> 00:22:51.290
and retry logic.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:53.300
So if a service is
down for a moment,

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.460
the gateway can retry
a couple of times,

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:58.910
and then the user
doesn't even know.

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:02.090
This is also a good place
to place perimeter security.

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:05.210
This allows you to
block certain users

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.520
to access any of your
downstream services

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.277
that you might not want to.

00:23:09.277 --> 00:23:11.360
And notice just that
perimeter security should not

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:15.960
be the only security that
you add to your application.

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:19.670
So how do we get a gateway in
the Springboard application?

00:23:19.670 --> 00:23:21.320
You basically just
add this dependency,

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:25.490
and you're able to now match
routes, write predicates

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:29.520
and filters, and set up a bunch
of things, which is really,

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.063
really fast and magical.

00:23:32.063 --> 00:23:33.980
And there's this other
thing called aggregator

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:35.130
or composite services.

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:37.130
These are basically just
services that aggregate

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:39.270
calls to other services.

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:40.850
This allows you
to maybe simplify

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:46.700
calls or implement things like
retry or get separate handling.

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:48.500
As an example, I told
you at the beginning

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:50.420
about this concept
of a flow in Louhi

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:53.810
that has notification
triggers in the pipeline.

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:56.600
And so instead of having
the front end talking

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:59.030
to these three services
to create just one flow,

00:23:59.030 --> 00:24:00.590
we created an
aggregating services

00:24:00.590 --> 00:24:01.940
called the flow service.

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:04.340
So now the front end can just
talk to the flow service,

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:06.220
and the flow service
handles logic

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:09.470
of talking to these other
services for the front end.

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:11.972
It also notices if a
service is down and maybe

00:24:11.972 --> 00:24:13.430
do a couple of
retries if something

00:24:13.430 --> 00:24:14.638
didn't happen as we expected.

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:19.290
Speaking of retries,
how do I even

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:24.060
configure the number of
retries in my service?

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.635
So when we were developing
our services in Louhi,

00:24:26.635 --> 00:24:28.260
we had a bunch of
configuration that we

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.730
wanted to add to our services--

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:33.360
retries, time outs,
feature flags.

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:36.750
And we didn't want to redeploy
our whole service when we

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.480
changed one of these variables.

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:42.540
Well, turns out that the
Spring Cloud Kubernetes project

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:45.990
allows us to basically push a
configuration map to our GKE

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:47.290
cluster.

00:24:47.290 --> 00:24:49.760
And as the cluster
notices these changes,

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:52.560
it delegates that
over to the services.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:55.740
And then you can just read
these as variables annotated

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:59.190
with the add value annotation
in your application--

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:03.300
really, really convenient
for toggling features.

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:06.570
SOEREN WALLS: Since
GlassPane doesn't use Spring,

00:25:06.570 --> 00:25:08.790
we didn't have this cool
configuration management

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:10.090
right out of the box.

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:13.110
So we decided to implement
this functionality ourselves.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:16.440
We created this
configuration microservice

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:19.330
that listens for changes
to a [? protobuf ?]

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:21.060
file, essentially
sitting somewhere

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:24.780
in a repository containing the
configuration settings for all

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.790
of our microservices
like feature flags

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:30.240
and a number of retries and the
things Sergio was mentioning.

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:33.990
When this configuration service
detects a change to this file,

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:36.570
it sends a message via
Cloud Pub/Sub to all

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:39.100
of the other GlassPane
microservices to tell them,

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:41.790
hey, the configuration
is updated.

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:44.850
And they know to update
those values without having

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:46.348
to be redeployed.

00:25:49.268 --> 00:25:51.310
SERGIO FELIX: OK so we
get a bunch of services up

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:53.890
and running, and
we might want to be

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:57.700
able to carry out complex
transactions across them.

00:25:57.700 --> 00:25:59.020
Let me set an example.

00:25:59.020 --> 00:26:00.910
Imagine this.

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:03.070
Imagine that you have
a project in Louhi,

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:05.800
and a project might have
additional metadata associated

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:07.090
with it.

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:10.480
And [? let's ?] pretend that a
user wants to delete a project.

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:14.607
So the project service gets a
message to delete project X.

00:26:14.607 --> 00:26:16.190
So the product service
is really easy.

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:19.210
It just goes to its data
store and deletes the project,

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:20.807
and that's it.

00:26:20.807 --> 00:26:22.390
There might be some
cleanup that needs

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:25.300
to happen from other services
if you deleted a project.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:27.310
Maybe you stopped the
triggers or if it's

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:29.000
a pipeline that's
currently running,

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:31.420
stop it or something like that.

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:35.173
And you want to be able to
do these atomically as well.

00:26:35.173 --> 00:26:37.340
And then on top of that,
imagine that in the future,

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:38.050
the system grows.

00:26:38.050 --> 00:26:40.640
And there's new services
that are available,

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:43.030
and they also need to
react to things that happen

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:45.010
when a project gets deleted.

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:47.060
How do you do that?

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:50.210
It turns out that there are some
patterns called saga patterns.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:54.960
I'll talk about orchestration
and choreography.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:57.070
In orchestration, you
have a single service that

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.960
coordinates what's happening.

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:00.910
These coordinators
service makes sure

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:03.310
that things happen
in a certain order

00:27:03.310 --> 00:27:05.735
or that they're even
happening at all.

00:27:05.735 --> 00:27:07.360
The problem with
orchestration, though,

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:10.300
is if this magical
coordinator service somehow

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:15.730
dies, the other services
don't know what to do.

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:17.470
And so then you
have choreography.

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:21.070
In choreography, services
basically produce and listen

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:24.400
to events and then
react accordingly.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:27.725
There is no single service
coordinating the actions.

00:27:27.725 --> 00:27:30.100
The downside to this is that
there might be some overhead

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.302
because you're sending
events, and all the messages

00:27:32.302 --> 00:27:33.010
have to get them.

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:37.870
But in Louhi, we use
a combination of both.

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:40.060
For the example that I
was just talking about,

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:43.000
the project service emits
an event saying, hey,

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:45.310
project X has been deleted.

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:47.157
And through eventual
consistency,

00:27:47.157 --> 00:27:49.240
the message arrives
eventually to all the services

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:51.080
who are interested
in this message

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:55.750
and then delete the project
in a fan out strategy.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:57.340
The opposite can also be done.

00:27:57.340 --> 00:28:00.220
A single service might be
listening to multiple events

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:01.730
from other services.

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:04.630
For example, we want to do this
with our metric service we.

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:07.510
Want to use a metric service
to calculate release velocity

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:10.435
and additional metrics
in our application.

00:28:13.598 --> 00:28:15.640
SOEREN WALLS: Sergio
covered some useful patterns

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:18.170
for coordinating your services.

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:20.800
But as your application
grows and you're maybe

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:24.430
using these aggregator
or composite services,

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:26.890
how do you keep track
of the requests that

00:28:26.890 --> 00:28:31.120
are being sent between all
of these different services?

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:35.290
Distributed tracing is a
technique to do exactly this.

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:37.750
Zipkin is an example
of a tool that Spring

00:28:37.750 --> 00:28:42.010
uses to add essentially
breadcrumbs to the HTTP headers

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:44.043
automatically to
all your requests,

00:28:44.043 --> 00:28:45.460
allowing you to
track them as they

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.630
bounce from service to service.

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:50.050
This means that you can
troubleshoot errors and gather

00:28:50.050 --> 00:28:53.710
metrics about the way that your
services are communicating--

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:55.510
really useful stuff.

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:59.170
As an alternative, there's
tools like Envoy and Istio

00:28:59.170 --> 00:29:02.710
that create a service
mesh to enhance

00:29:02.710 --> 00:29:07.120
HTTP requests between services
running on any tech stack

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:09.760
to provide your application
with distributed tracing

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:13.338
capabilities automatically.

00:29:13.338 --> 00:29:14.880
SERGIO FELIX: So,
for Louhi, in order

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:16.800
to implement distributed
tracing we only

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.542
had to add these two
dependencies to our services

00:29:19.542 --> 00:29:21.750
after this our Springboard
applications began pushing

00:29:21.750 --> 00:29:23.850
information to Stackdriver.

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:25.440
That meant that we
can then go back

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:28.170
to our Stackdriver
in our GCP console,

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:31.230
and we could actually
tell and see the messages

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:33.803
bouncing across
all our services.

00:29:33.803 --> 00:29:35.220
And if you wanted
to, you can even

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:37.860
click a button to go into
detail about what's happening

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:39.300
in each individual service.

00:29:43.310 --> 00:29:45.060
How did the services
even find each other?

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:47.220
If I create a new
service, how do the others

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:49.980
know that it even exists?

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:52.478
Through this thing called
service discoverability.

00:29:52.478 --> 00:29:54.270
It's been around for
a while, but it really

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:58.260
became popular for microservices
when Netflix introduced Eureka.

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:00.130
The concept is
actually pretty simple.

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:02.280
You have something called
a service registry.

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:05.250
When new services arrive, they
talk to the service registry

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:06.840
and say, hey, I'm the service.

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:08.790
I'm the product service.

00:30:08.790 --> 00:30:11.250
And then other services can
query the service registry

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:14.420
to find out what other
services are available.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:17.730
And you can do some really
interesting things with this.

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:19.960
I'll show an example here.

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:24.000
In Louhi, we had this general
trigger service and maybe

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.350
some implementations, a manual
trigger and a git trigger.

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:30.173
And we wanted to have
a way to enhance Louhi

00:30:30.173 --> 00:30:32.340
to have new types of triggers
without actually going

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:35.153
back and changing the
existing source code.

00:30:35.153 --> 00:30:36.820
In order to do this,
we leveraged server

00:30:36.820 --> 00:30:38.430
discoverability.

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:40.950
If you wanted to add
maybe a Pub/Sub trigger,

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:44.030
we just wrote the
Pub/Sub trigger in a way,

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:47.040
drop it into the
cluster, the service

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:49.740
register with
Kubernetes, and then

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:52.890
the trigger service became
aware that this service actually

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:54.203
existed now.

00:30:54.203 --> 00:30:55.620
With that, the
trigger service can

00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:58.780
go back to the Pub/Sub trigger
and interrogate the server

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:00.990
the service asking what it did.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:02.847
And then it could expose
that functionality

00:31:02.847 --> 00:31:03.930
across the other services.

00:31:07.170 --> 00:31:09.810
But what if a service goes down?

00:31:09.810 --> 00:31:12.870
Unlike monolithic apps, one
major benefit of microservices

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:14.670
is that your whole
application can somewhat

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:18.690
be up and running even when one
of those services goes down.

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:21.058
How do you handle this?

00:31:21.058 --> 00:31:23.100
I'll talk about some
patterns, behavior patterns,

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:25.267
circuit breaker patterns,
and self-healing services.

00:31:27.862 --> 00:31:29.570
So a circuit breaker
pattern is basically

00:31:29.570 --> 00:31:31.130
a pattern used to
detect failures

00:31:31.130 --> 00:31:35.300
and allows your services
to react to those failures.

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:36.830
Here's an example.

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:39.110
Imagine that a user
wants to do something

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:40.580
with the flow service.

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:42.800
And in order for that to
happen, the flow service

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:45.050
needs to talk to
the trigger service.

00:31:45.050 --> 00:31:47.120
If everything is
fine, the flow service

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:50.430
talks to the trigger
service, and that's it.

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:54.708
Imagine that for some reason,
the trigger services down.

00:31:54.708 --> 00:31:57.000
The user doesn't know that
the trigger service is down,

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:59.040
obviously, and so
maybe the user will

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:02.020
click a button to try again.

00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:05.520
Maybe the user's impatient
and clicks the button again

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:08.730
and again and again.

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:11.970
And then you run into this
issue where the flow service

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:15.390
has a bunch of open connections
waiting for the trigger servers

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:16.650
to come back.

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:18.990
And then the trigger
service is dead.

00:32:18.990 --> 00:32:21.320
And so if it ever
becomes available again,

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:23.050
it doesn't know all
those other requests,

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:25.980
which are in progress.

00:32:25.980 --> 00:32:28.440
And the user is super
confused because the user

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:31.410
doesn't know what's happening.

00:32:31.410 --> 00:32:34.190
So here is the same thing
but with a circuit breaker.

00:32:34.190 --> 00:32:37.610
Again, if nothing's
wrong, the flow service

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:40.370
through the breaker goes
to the trigger service.

00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:43.080
Everything's fine.

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:46.390
The [INAUDIBLE] end,
and the user's happy.

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:49.180
But again, let's say that
the trigger service is down

00:32:49.180 --> 00:32:51.110
for some reason.

00:32:51.110 --> 00:32:54.130
The flow service goes
through the circuit breaker

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:58.150
onto the trigger service,
and now the circuit breaker

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:01.180
will try a couple of
times for you, depending

00:33:01.180 --> 00:33:03.430
on how you've configured this.

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:04.960
When the circuit
breaker determines

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:07.730
that the trigger
service is unhealthy,

00:33:07.730 --> 00:33:10.600
it will get a chance to react.

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:13.330
The circuit is open, and
now the circuit breaker

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:16.910
can actually throw out an
exception in your flow service.

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:19.930
And now, your fellow
service can handle logic.

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:22.600
Should you maybe tell the
user that something's wrong?

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:25.480
Maybe it'll store the request
and try again after the trigger

00:33:25.480 --> 00:33:28.648
service becomes available.

00:33:28.648 --> 00:33:30.690
SOEREN WALLS: So Sergio
threw the pattern at you.

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:33.430
Now when I show you how can
you actually implement this.

00:33:33.430 --> 00:33:36.360
So we did this in
GlassPane, and here's how.

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:39.690
We had this metric
service in Java

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:42.390
that attempts to get all the
available metrics in GlassPane

00:33:42.390 --> 00:33:46.110
by sending a request to
the metric microservice.

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:47.860
But what happens if that fails?

00:33:47.860 --> 00:33:51.510
Well, as you can see here,
it just throws an exception,

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:53.250
and the user doesn't
know what happened.

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:54.850
Where are my metrics?

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:57.780
Well, we implemented
this by creating

00:33:57.780 --> 00:34:01.290
a new annotation called the
method retry annotation.

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:04.140
As you can see, it takes
in a number of attempts

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:08.610
to attempt the method and
even a fallback method

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:11.730
so that when that number
of attempts is surpassed,

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:14.585
it actually can fall
back and say, hey,

00:34:14.585 --> 00:34:15.960
we weren't able
to get a request.

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:18.420
But here we're going to pull
the metrics from the local

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:21.989
repository in this service--
maybe from previous requests--

00:34:21.989 --> 00:34:24.659
and get some default
metrics to show the user.

00:34:24.659 --> 00:34:29.620
And maybe we also show an
error message on the UI.

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:32.940
This is just one way you
can implement this pattern,

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:35.280
but this is the kind
of cool flexibility

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:36.810
that's possible
with microservices.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.310
Depending on your
tech stack, this

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:43.102
might be provided automatically,
or you can create it yourself.

00:34:43.102 --> 00:34:45.060
SERGIO FELIX: And then
there's this other thing

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:47.219
call self-healing services.

00:34:47.219 --> 00:34:49.320
For example, Kubernetes
is able to monitor

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:52.290
the state or your health of
your application to find out

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:53.889
if it's down or not.

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:57.090
And if it is, Kubernetes
able to kill the plot

00:34:57.090 --> 00:34:59.790
and create a new instance
of your applications

00:34:59.790 --> 00:35:02.622
so that it makes sure that
is always up and running.

00:35:02.622 --> 00:35:04.080
However, for this
to work, you need

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:06.495
to have a way for your
application to tell Kubernetes

00:35:06.495 --> 00:35:08.580
that it's actually alive.

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:09.960
And in Springbopard
applications,

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:12.940
you can actually use this
Spring Starter Actuator.

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:15.450
The actuator basically adds
additional endpoints to report

00:35:15.450 --> 00:35:17.762
the health of your application.

00:35:17.762 --> 00:35:19.470
And if configured
correctly, you can even

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:23.873
use something called
micrometer to gather metrics

00:35:23.873 --> 00:35:26.040
about traffic coming back
and forth in your service.

00:35:28.372 --> 00:35:30.080
Also, I didn't want
to leave you hanging.

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:33.850
You can also use circuit
breaker pattern and service

00:35:33.850 --> 00:35:36.410
discoverability in Spring.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:39.705
You basically have [? fame ?]
clients that you use.

00:35:39.705 --> 00:35:41.330
Notice that I don't
have an IP address.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:43.790
You just basically name what
client you want to talk to,

00:35:43.790 --> 00:35:45.500
and it goes back to
the service registry

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:48.401
and finds out who's the service
is, where it lives, and so on.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.650
SOEREN WALLS: All
right, it sounds

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:56.620
like there's some cool
benefits to microservices,

00:35:56.620 --> 00:36:00.690
but having all of these
different binaries

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:02.250
sounds toilsome.

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:05.220
How do you manage
all of this stuff?

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:08.490
Building, testing, and
deploying all of these services

00:36:08.490 --> 00:36:11.520
can sound like a
big, daunting task--

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:13.990
maybe as big as a
monolithic app itself.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:16.950
How do you manage
these services?

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:19.290
Automation is your friend.

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:21.090
There are tools out
there that we really

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:23.010
encourage you to
use that will help

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:27.680
to automate the different parts
of managing your services.

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:32.010
This can be in a CI/CD
pipeline to make it

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:35.130
so that you're deploying,
testing continuously

00:36:35.130 --> 00:36:36.152
and automatically.

00:36:36.152 --> 00:36:37.860
If you're trying to
do all this manually,

00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:39.110
it's not going to be feasible.

00:36:41.283 --> 00:36:43.200
SERGIO FELIX: So, those
things that we clearly

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:44.850
didn't cover in
this presentation

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:48.468
but we encourage you to go
out and search for this.

00:36:48.468 --> 00:36:50.130
As Soeren mentioned,
you want to be

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:52.380
able to automate the creation
of your infrastructure--

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:54.005
either through code
or configuration.

00:36:54.005 --> 00:36:55.380
There are lots of
tools out there

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:57.870
like Ansible, Puppet, Chef.

00:36:57.870 --> 00:37:01.110
Google Cloud and Amazon
have implementations

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:01.917
for these things.

00:37:01.917 --> 00:37:03.750
In Louhi, we use something
called Terraform.

00:37:07.450 --> 00:37:10.270
You want to have a CI/CD
pipeline for microservice.

00:37:10.270 --> 00:37:12.330
Make sure that you
always run test

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:17.642
whenever a change gets pushed
to one of your repositories.

00:37:17.642 --> 00:37:19.100
Have a rollout
strategy-- you think

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:21.220
about how you're going to
deploy the services, how

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:22.220
you want to deploy them.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:25.940
Always when the test passes, are
you're going to have a cannery?

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:28.250
Are you going to do
green blue deployments?

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:30.290
Think about that.

00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:34.550
Set up error logging
metrics and reporting--

00:37:34.550 --> 00:37:36.680
you want to do this
at the beginning

00:37:36.680 --> 00:37:38.698
when you're developing
the services instead

00:37:38.698 --> 00:37:39.740
of waiting until the end.

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:45.200
Obviously, don't forget
about security and testing.

00:37:45.200 --> 00:37:46.630
We didn't cover
this topic at all,

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:50.670
but we encourage you
to go out and look.

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:52.830
SOEREN WALLS: So what
can you take away

00:37:52.830 --> 00:37:54.570
from this presentation?

00:37:54.570 --> 00:37:57.570
Well, earlier, we talked
about some of the pros

00:37:57.570 --> 00:38:01.650
and cons of monolithic apps
versus microservices and how

00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:04.290
microservices came to be.

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:07.320
We told you why we initially
built Louhi and GlassPane

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:09.810
as monolithic apps
and why we decided

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:12.930
to go and push into this great
adventure of converting them

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:16.620
into microservices, despite
the great refactoring costs.

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:19.950
Microservices allowed us to
iterate quickly and focus

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:21.360
on solving our users' needs.

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:25.078
So ultimately, we got
out of it what we wanted.

00:38:25.078 --> 00:38:26.870
SERGIO FELIX: Throughout
this presentation,

00:38:26.870 --> 00:38:28.600
we also showed you
how to take advantage

00:38:28.600 --> 00:38:31.090
of existing patterns like
the strangled pattern

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:34.072
to slowly break
down your service.

00:38:34.072 --> 00:38:35.530
We also talked
about cycle patterns

00:38:35.530 --> 00:38:38.320
to reinforce atomic operations,
circuit breaker when something

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:39.735
goes down.

00:38:39.735 --> 00:38:41.360
We used examples of
Louhi and GlassPane

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:42.735
to show you how
you can implement

00:38:42.735 --> 00:38:46.985
the same patterns with
different technology stacks.

00:38:46.985 --> 00:38:48.360
SOEREN WALLS:
Ultimately, there's

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:52.930
no one size fits all approach
to the perfect application.

00:38:52.930 --> 00:38:56.190
If there were, we
wouldn't be here.

00:38:56.190 --> 00:38:58.440
There would be no
point for us to talk.

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:01.740
But we hope that you
learned from our experience

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:03.990
with Louhi and GlassPane
and that you took away

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:07.170
some useful tips for how to
choose a good architecture

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:08.850
for your app.

00:39:08.850 --> 00:39:11.892
We hope the road ahead
is a little clearer now.

00:39:11.892 --> 00:39:13.350
There's this cool
search engine you

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:16.590
can use to search for all the
tools and things that we talked

00:39:16.590 --> 00:39:21.880
about in this presentation
in case anything is vague.

00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:24.540
But we wish you the best
of luck on your journey.

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:27.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

